c0370
00:58:15.582 --> 00:58:32.582
I wonder if you can comment on the Magid. Was it a real Magid, a Shliach from heaven, or it was an auto-suggestion, or maybe it was just an echo of his thought?

c0371
00:58:32.582 --> 00:58:43.582
Well, this is a very difficult question to answer. I know that some people, there is a book written on Joseph Qaro in which this subject is analyzed, and some people say that, I mean,

c0372
00:58:43.582 --> 00:58:58.582
Briefly, let me tell you, according to our cultural standard today, if somebody in our culture would say an angel is talking to me, he will be considered a mildly schizophrenic.

c0373
00:58:58.582 --> 00:59:10.582
But as psychologists teach us, madness and sanity is conditioned by culture.

c0374
00:59:10.582 --> 00:59:18.582
And what in one culture could be considered as mad, in another culture is considered as totally normal.

c0375
00:59:18.582 --> 00:59:21.582
And not only in question of time, but also in space.

c0376
00:59:21.582 --> 00:59:34.582
In this space, for instance, would you consider some of the behavior, say, of people in India, Japan, China, Australian Aborigines, American Indians?

c0377
00:59:34.582 --> 00:59:42.582
If you compare it to what we do, well, we would say that person maybe is not considered normal from our standard.

c0378
00:59:42.582 --> 00:59:50.582
But psychologists say that we have to be a little bit more relativistic and realize that sanity and normalness,

c0379
00:59:50.582 --> 00:59:59.982
Whatever is normal, it is conditioned by time and society. So again I would say,

c0380
01:00:00.000 --> 01:00:06.440
Whether he was a saint or not, or this, I mean, I cannot answer. I'm not qualified.

c0381
01:00:06.440 --> 01:00:13.920
The only thing I could say, that in that time, in that society, visions with angels was considered normal.

c0382
01:00:13.920 --> 01:00:16.400
That's the only thing I could answer, but I cannot go more than that.

c0383
01:00:16.400 --> 01:00:21.040
Is there any traces in the Shulhan 'Arukh, any mystical traces?

c0384
01:00:21.040 --> 01:00:25.600
Very important question. This is a very, very, I appreciate the question, because really it is.

c0385
01:00:25.600 --> 01:00:36.600
One of the important elements, although the Shukran Aruch himself was a mystic and believed in mysticism, you find zero trace of mysticism in the Shukran Aruch.

c0386
01:00:36.600 --> 01:00:46.600
He really was able to control whatever mystical ideals had and to put it, to leave it only in the realm of the very very private.

c0387
01:00:46.600 --> 01:00:54.600
In point of fact, his book Magid Mishalim, he did not publish. It was published after his death by his students.

c0388
01:00:54.600 --> 01:01:02.000
Yes? So, he was, he, this, for him, the mysticism was a personal experience.

c0389
01:01:02.000 --> 01:01:06.400
And he was not interested in sharing this with the rest of the world.

c0390
01:01:06.400 --> 01:01:10.400
And the Shokan Rukh have no traces of it. None.

c0391
01:01:10.400 --> 01:01:11.400
Thank you.

c0392
01:01:15.400 --> 01:01:16.400
Two questions, please.

c0393
01:01:16.400 --> 01:01:22.000
First of all, to what extent do you think Rabbi Karo expected his treatise to be considered normative for the future,

c0394
01:01:22.000 --> 01:01:24.000
as opposed to just the period in which he left?

c0395
01:01:24.000 --> 01:01:29.600
And secondly, to the extent that it was supported because of its economic, political, and social implications,

c0396
01:01:29.600 --> 01:01:33.600
do you see that as a possible model for resolving some of the issues in Israel today?

c0397
01:01:33.600 --> 01:01:38.000
Well, let me begin with an important observation.

c0398
01:01:38.000 --> 01:01:48.000
The decay of, or the loss of authority of the Shulhan 'Arukh comes exactly at the time when Napoleon establishes his international law,

c0399
01:01:48.000 --> 01:01:54.400
And therefore the Jew, rather than to rely on the Shulhan 'Arukh, he relies on international law to do his business.

c0400
01:01:54.400 --> 01:02:01.600
So here you see that there was a relationship, a very strong relationship between pragmatics and Tora.

c0401
01:02:01.600 --> 01:02:10.400
And that you see very clearly. As soon as the Jews had other courts besides the Jewish court,

c0402
01:02:10.400 --> 01:02:17.200
then they preferred, they took business and if you have a claim you would use a regular, normal, private international law.

c0403
01:02:17.200 --> 01:02:36.012
That what you apply Now what is important I believe of the Shulhan 'Arukh the two things I think is important Number one that every generation or every couple of generations they should have a code

c0404
01:02:36.012 --> 01:02:45.012
And the law should not be left to the arbitrary whims of the clergy. This I think is terribly important.

c0405
01:02:45.012 --> 01:02:51.012
Because otherwise, there is a possibility of abuse or the appearance of abuse.

c0406
01:02:51.012 --> 01:03:11.512
I think that today, even today, where many Jews who are committed ideologically to Jewish law, they find it a little bit uncomfortable to submit to what appears the whims of people who somehow they're appointed or self-appointed legal authorities.

c0407
01:03:11.512 --> 01:03:17.512
So that point, I think, of the Yosef Qaro is very important. We should have a Jewish code.

c0408
01:03:17.512 --> 01:03:28.512
Second, the Jewish code should not reflect one single Jewish community, but should reflect all of Jewish communities.

c0409
01:03:28.512 --> 01:03:33.512
In Jewish communities sometimes they should compromise among themselves. Yes?

c0410
01:03:33.512 --> 01:03:36.512
So I'm not going to have my feeling the way I want it, I'm not going to have it the way you want it.

c0411
01:03:36.512 --> 01:03:40.512
Now, I compromise in my feeling because of you, you compromise in the talents because of me.

c0412
01:03:40.512 --> 01:03:45.512
That's the way it is. We have to make some sort of compromise arrangements.

c0413
01:03:45.512 --> 01:03:49.512
And these two points, I believe, are of great value.

c0414
01:03:49.512 --> 01:04:00.512
Last but not least, I hope that one day, while we're still alive, we should renew the whole ideal of Desmicha.

c0415
01:04:00.512 --> 01:04:10.512
We'd have a proper Jewish Sanhedrin, representing all Jewish traditions and ideologies.

c0416
01:04:10.512 --> 01:04:15.512
And together, they should decide what Jewish law is all about.

c0417
01:04:17.512 --> 01:04:19.512
Thank you.

c0418
01:04:21.512 --> 01:04:29.512
Professor, didn't the reliance of the Orthodox upon the Shulhan 'Arukh create the stagnation

c0419
01:04:29.512 --> 01:04:35.512
where the Orthodox would not make any new amendments as had been going on for thousands of years?

c0420
01:04:35.512 --> 01:05:17.024
Well let me respond to you One of the early articles that are written by me when I came to this country was to show that in the Sepharadi tradition they circumvent developed the points of the Shukha Haruch constantly Forty years after the Shulhan 'Arukh there were already developments And very significant developments And you find paradoxically the greatest growth in Jewish legal literature comes from the Sepharadi traditions

c0421
01:05:17.024 --> 01:05:23.024
in specifically from those communities in which the Shulhan 'Arukh was considered as legally binding.

c0422
01:05:23.024 --> 01:05:30.024
Shulhan 'Arukh is a kind of a generative kind of work in which generates more knowledge.

c0423
01:05:30.024 --> 01:05:38.024
The reason why certain segments were stagnant is because they wanted to be stagnant.

c0424
01:05:38.024 --> 01:05:52.024
And they used the Shulhan 'Arukh or what have you as pretext to hide away from reality and to prevent Jewish progress.

c0425
01:05:52.024 --> 01:06:02.024
The Shukran al-Rukh himself, in his own responsa, very intelligently, departs in many cases from the Shukran al-Rukh.

c0426
01:06:02.024 --> 01:06:09.024
Because from the time that he wrote the Shukran al-Rukh until the time the responsum came to his desk, some time had lapsed.

c0427
01:06:09.024 --> 01:06:13.024
And already he was ready, and he did make changes.

c0428
01:06:13.024 --> 01:06:23.024
There is another book, which I did not mention, it is called Bedeq Abayit, in which he makes the final revisions right before he dies.

c0429
01:06:23.024 --> 01:06:37.024
So, a great legal mind is constantly evolving. The Shukran Arush is supposed to be a pattern that you could accept and as a challenge try to improve.

c0430
01:06:37.024 --> 01:06:40.464
And if you improve, all the best and all the better.

c0431
01:06:41.184 --> 01:06:47.904
The reason why there is a stagnation in Jewish legal thought,

c0432
01:06:48.704 --> 01:06:53.344
it is not because the Jewish legal system demands stagnation,

c0433
01:06:53.584 --> 01:07:01.824
but rather because those who proclaim themselves as the spokesmen for the legal system want to be stagnant.

c0434
01:07:01.824 --> 01:07:08.824
Thank you very... oh, that's it.

